{
  "dom_id": "step-q84696",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A fair spinner is designed so that $30%$ of its surface area is painted in red. Tom spins the spinner $15$ times. What is the standard deviation of the number of times the spinner lands on red? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner lands on red.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ spins\n\n- the probability of landing on red is $p=30%=0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(15,0.3).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times the spinner lands on red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(15)(0.3)(1-0.3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3.15} \\\\  & =1.775\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
